    <section id="overview"><h2>From token IDs to contextual representations</h2>
<p>Transformers operate on sequences of token IDs. A tokenizer splits text into words, subwords or other units; an embedding layer maps IDs to vectors. Because self-attention alone does not encode token order, positional information is added to token embeddings.</p><p>Unlike a recurrent model, a Transformer can process many sequence positions in parallel during training. Self-attention allows each token representation to combine information from other positions, subject to any attention mask.</p>
</section>
<section id="attention"><h2>Scaled dot-product self-attention</h2>
<p>For an input representation matrix \(X\), learned projections create queries, keys and values:</p><div class="eq">\(Q=XW_Q,\quad K=XW_K,\quad V=XW_V\)</div><div class="eq">\(\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)=\operatorname{softmax}(QK^T/\sqrt{d_k})V\)</div><p>Queries express what a token is looking for; keys describe what each token offers for matching; values carry the information to combine. Dividing by \(\sqrt{d_k}\) keeps dot-product magnitudes controlled as key dimension grows. The softmax is applied across key positions.</p><p><strong>Multi-head attention</strong> runs several attention projections in parallel, allowing different heads to learn different relationships, then combines their outputs.</p>
</section>
<section id="architecture"><h2>Transformer blocks and masking</h2>
<p>A standard Transformer block includes multi-head attention, a feed-forward network, residual connections and layer normalization. Dropout may also be used. Residual connections add a sublayer's input to its output, helping information and gradients travel through deep stacks.</p><ul><li><strong>Encoder self-attention:</strong> each source token can usually attend to all source positions.</li><li><strong>Causal decoder self-attention:</strong> a token may attend only to itself and earlier positions, preventing access to future target tokens.</li><li><strong>Cross-attention:</strong> in encoder-decoder models, decoder queries attend to encoder representations.</li></ul><p>Padding masks hide artificial pad positions. A causal mask and a padding mask solve different problems.</p>
</section>
<section id="language-models"><h2>Encoder, decoder and encoder-decoder use cases</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Typical behaviour</th><th>Examples of tasks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Encoder-only</td><td>Bidirectional contextual representations</td><td>Classification, token labelling, retrieval embeddings</td></tr><tr><td>Decoder-only</td><td>Autoregressive next-token prediction</td><td>Text generation and chat</td></tr><tr><td>Encoder-decoder</td><td>Encode an input, then generate an output sequence</td><td>Translation, summarization, text transformation</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Tokenization, vocabulary, context length, padding/truncation and decoding settings can all affect model outputs. Hugging Face pipelines provide a convenient high-level interface for common tasks, while direct model/tokenizer APIs offer more control.</p><p>Common pipeline tasks demonstrated in the supplied inference notebook include <strong>sentiment analysis</strong> (classifying text polarity), <strong>named entity recognition (NER)</strong> (locating spans such as people, organizations and places), and <strong>translation</strong> (generating a target-language sequence). A pipeline name does not make every model suitable for every task: check the model card, supported languages, label definitions and input limits.</p><pre><code>from transformers import pipeline
sentiment = pipeline("sentiment-analysis")
print(sentiment("The explanation was clear and helpful."))</code></pre><p>The pipeline typically handles tokenization, model inference and decoding/postprocessing. Results depend on the selected pretrained model and its training data; do not interpret a confidence score as a guarantee of correctness.</p>
</section>
<section id="questions"><h2>Week 10 questions and solutions</h2>
<p>Original exam-style practice based on the supplied Transformer and Hugging Face materials.</p><details class="question"><summary>Q1. Why are positional encodings or positional embeddings needed in a Transformer?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Self-attention needs added information about token order.</p><p>Without positional information, attention's content-based operations alone do not distinguish sequence order in the required way.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q2. In attention, what do Q, K and V represent?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Queries, keys and values.</p><p>Query-key similarity determines attention weights; those weights combine the value vectors.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q3. Why divide QKᵀ by √d_k?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> To control score scale as key dimension increases.</p><p>Large dot products can make softmax very peaked and gradients small; scaling helps stabilize the scores.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q4. What prevents a decoder language model from seeing future target tokens during training?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> A causal (look-ahead) attention mask.</p><p>The mask blocks attention to positions after the current token.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q5. What is the purpose of multi-head attention?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Allow different attention heads to learn different relationships in parallel.</p><p>Each head uses its own projections before the outputs are combined.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q6. Which Transformer family is naturally suited to autoregressive text generation?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Decoder-only.</p><p>It predicts the next token using prior tokens and a causal attention mask.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q7. What is cross-attention in an encoder-decoder Transformer?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Decoder queries attend to encoder output representations.</p><p>It lets generated target tokens condition on the encoded source sequence.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q8. What does a Hugging Face text-generation pipeline simplify?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Loading a compatible model/tokenizer and running generation through a high-level API.</p><p>The pipeline wraps common preprocessing, model inference and postprocessing steps.</p></div></details>
</section>

